WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.550
With allusions to previous MCU movies and
references to comics that haven't made their

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.330
way to the big screen, Avengers: Endgame reminds
Marvel fans that the people behind the camera

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.980
are just as enthusiastic as the ones filling
the seats.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.580
Here are some lines in Avengers: Endgame that
were more important than you may have realized.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:20.500
If you saw Avengers: Endgame in a packed theater,
then the moment Thor's hammer Mjolnir flies

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.320
into Captain America's hand probably inspired
one of the loudest and most enthusiastic responses

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.100
from the audience.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.120
If not, then it's at least met with enthusiasm
from Thor himself, who yells "I knew it!"

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.240
as he spots Cap threatening Thanos with the
legendary weapon.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.540
So what did Thor mean by "I knew it?"

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.780
In an early scene in 2015's Avengers: Age
of Ultron, Thor gives the rest of the team

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.449
permission to try to lift Mjolnir.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.879
Thor sits back, confident none of his mere
mortal teammates will prove worthy to wield

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.780
the hammer… until Steve Rogers gives it
a shot.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.280
That brief nudge, and past comic book stories
in which Cap has proven worthy of Mjolnir,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.520
left some fans theorizing that Cap could've
lifted the hammer but chose not to in order

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.520
to spare his teammate any humiliation.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.160
Apparently, the Odinson was one of those fans.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.210
When Thor yells "I knew it!" he betrays that
he thought Cap was faking way back in 2015

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:15.940
as well.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.060
And how couldn't he?

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.380
Cap's as worthy as they come.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.640
Speaking of Thor and what he may or may not
be worthy to wield, the thunder god initially

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.830
thinks he's the only one capable of using
the new Infinity Gauntlet to bring back everyone

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.120
Thanos purged in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.180
The team argues about it and eventually it's
the Hulk who convinces everyone he's the only

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:33.840
guy for the job.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.180
As he does, it's made clear he's thinking
about a conversation he had seven years before

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.020
with Tony Stark.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.620
In Avengers, as Stark and Bruce Banner look
for a way to track the Tesseract, Downey brings

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.540
up Banner's "Other Guy" and compares it to
the Arc Reactor, which at the time was still

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.920
the only thing stopping shrapnel in Stark's
chest from killing him.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.719
Banner understands Stark’s suggestion that
the Hulk saved Banner’s life in the gamma

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.420
ray explosion, but isn’t totally on board
with the idea.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.020
“It's a nice sentiment.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.350
Saved it for what?

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.840
“I guess we'll find out."

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.619
In Avengers: Endgame, he finally finds out
what he was saved for.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:11.930
Explaining that he's the only one who can
wield the Stones and that their radiation

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.060
is mostly gamma ...the same as the radiation
that turned him into the Hulk ...Banner says,

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.940
quote, "It's like I was made for this."

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:24.420
When Hulk goes back in time to 2012's Battle
of New York, his assignment is to retrieve

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.420
the Eye of Agamotto, a.k.a. the Time Stone.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.860
Instead of Doctor Strange, the Hulk finds
the Ancient One waiting for him at the Sanctum

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.400
Sanctorum.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.440
Banner and the Ancient One argue over whether
or not it makes sense for her to risk her

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.400
own timeline by handing over the Stone.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.260
It's when the Ancient One learns that Strange
gave the Time Stone to Thanos that she changes

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:39.800
her mind.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.760
She tells Banner, quote, "Strange is meant
to be the best of us," as she gives him the

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.420
Time Stone, meaning that she realizes her
future student wouldn't surrender the Stone

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.040
unless there was a good reason.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.000
Considering what happens in 2016's Doctor
Strange, the Ancient One's reaction is interesting.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:58.060
Four years from when she calls him "the best
of us," she'll have Strange physically thrown

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.220
out of Kamar-taj.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:00.440
"Teach me …

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.140
No."

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:10.560
You could call this a contradiction, but it
could also suggest a lot more complexity to

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.640
the early Sorcerer Supreme and her decisions.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.580
Did she expect Strange to eventually show
up and wrestle with the decision of whether

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.689
or not to let him in?

00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:21.630
Or perhaps ...sensing Mordo's potential for
turning on his allies ...did she kick Strange

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.540
out just to see what Mordo's reaction would
be?

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.660
Shortly after Tony Stark figures out time
travel, he settles down on a couch with Pepper

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.540
and talks about what he should do next.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.750
He tells her that part of him wants scrap
all his work, box it up, and drop it to "the

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.260
bottom of the lake."

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:39.460
This is the same Pepper who almost left Tony's
employ in 2008's Iron Man over his armored

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.280
adventures and who was ready to break up with
him over his constant "tinkering" in 2013's

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:44.740
Iron Man 3.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.120
"This is a new level of lame!"

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.860
She clearly developed a sense of resigned
acceptance since we last saw her, because

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.700
her words seem to be what convince Tony to
take his new innovation to the Avengers.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.219
After listening to her husband, Pepper tells
him that trying to stop him has been one of

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.740
the few great failures in her life.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.980
It's accurate, as anyone who has seen all
the Iron Man entries can testify, but there's

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.580
a bittersweet irony you don't see until the
end of the movie's climactic battle.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.480
After Tony uses the Infinity Stones to wipe
out Thanos and his forces, he collapses and

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.920
struggles to hold on to what little life he
has left.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.160
Pepper wills herself to smile at her dying
husband and tells him, quote, "You can rest

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:19.680
now."

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.520
And so he does.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.100
Finally and tragically, Pepper gets Tony to
stop.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.180
When Captain America proves himself worthy
of Mjolnir, it isn't the only time in the

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.980
final battle that the old soldier has fans
clapping and cheering.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.390
After the heroes Thanos purged in Avengers:
Infinity War finally appear ...along with

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:38.470
the forces of Wakanda, Kamar-taj, and New
Asgard ...Cap takes his place at the head

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.180
of this army of resurrected friends.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:45.050
He leads the charge against Thanos' dark forces
with the line comic book fans have read so

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:45.840
often, "Avengers…

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:46.360
Assemble!"

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.720
You may not even realize it until that moment,
but we've been waiting for over ten years

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.220
to hear Cap say it.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.720
He may have led the team in 2012's Avengers,
but he never said the line.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.260
He even teased us with it at the close of
Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.820
"Avengers…."

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.740
When Cap finally says the line in Endgame
...if you hadn't realized it before ...it

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.490
suddenly occurs to you the MCU architects
waited a long time for that line, and man,

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.200
was it worth the wait.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:16.220
It's Emma Fuhrmann playing Scott Lang's daughter
Cassie in Avengers: Endgame ...not the younger

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.970
Abby Ryder Fortson, who plays her in Ant-Man
and Ant-Man &amp; The Wasp ...but that doesn't

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:23.280
stop Scott from recognizing her when he returns
from the Quantum Realm.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.580
At the end of their reunion scene, Scott says,
"You're so big!"

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.350
It seems fairly obvious ...and it's something
adults say to kids all the time if they haven't

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:31.410
seen them for a bit.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.199
"You’re so big and so well grown."

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:36.250
But it has a double meaning understood by
fans of the comics.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:40.520
In 2004, the comic book version of Scott Lang
was seemingly one of the fatal victims of

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.380
Marvel's Avengers Disassembled event.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.060
In the wake of his death, his daughter becomes
the hero Stature as part of Young Avengers.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.260
Like her father, she has powers that can make
her smaller or bigger.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.199
In a tragic twist, Stature dies just after
her father is brought back to life in 2010's

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.300
Avengers: The Children's Crusade.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.400
Thankfully, Cassie rejoins the land of the
living and is reunited with her father in

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.190
2015's Avengers World #16 because, you know.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:07.040
She's a Marvel Comics character, so it's pretty
much mandatory.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.680
After Thanos unleashes his assault on the
Avengers compound, Hawkeye is the first to

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.340
get his hands on the new Infinity Gauntlet.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.840
He holds onto the thing for a while, fighting
off a swarm of Outriders and surviving an

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.820
attempt to steal it by the 2014 version of
Nebula.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:24.360
But once the purged heroes return and things
get real, Hawkeye calls for help.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.240
The first hero to respond is Black Panther,
who calls out, "Clint!

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:29.120
Give it to me!"

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.320
It's not a particularly memorable line until
you remember how these characters met and

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.940
the fact that they only exchanged two lines
of dialogue before Endgame.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.080
In particular, their entire conversation was
about Hawkeye's real name and how little it

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.720
mattered, so it's kind of funny that's the
name Black Panther uses in Endgame.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.449
In 2016's Captain America: Civil War.. during
the airport battle and shortly after Ant-Man

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:51.440
shocks the other heroes by turning giant instead
of tiny..

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.360
T'Challa advances on Hawkeye.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.660
The conversation that follows is short and
to the point.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:57.780
"We haven’t met yet.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.160
I’m Clint."

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:02.200
"I don’t care."

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.760
Apparently, he started caring just a little
more by the time Endgame rolled around.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:10.580
One of the best action sequences in 2014's
Captain America: The Winter Soldier is the

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.630
tense and eventually violent elevator scene,
as S.H.I.E.L.D. operatives ...who are actually

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:19.470
Hydra agents ...slowly fill an elevator Steve
Rogers is taking, and the old soldier soon

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.830
realizes he's walked into a death trap.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.330
Avengers: Endgame gives fans a scene acting
as both sequel and prequel, as it takes place

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.319
moments after Loki's capture in 2012's Avengers.

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:32.740
S.H.I.E.L.D. agents are transporting Loki's
scepter when the Captain America from the

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.130
future intercepts them in the elevator.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.930
He's able to convince them to hand over the
scepter when he whispers, "Hail Hydra."

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:43.190
The line references the Marvel Comics storyline
that introduced a version of Captain America

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.129
who was secretly serving Hydra.

00:07:45.129 --> 00:07:49.629
This was extremely controversial, and Captain
America: Steve Rogers writer Nick Spencer

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:51.710
received death threats over it.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:57.000
Hydra Cap is eventually defeated at the end
of the 2017 line-wide event Secret Empire.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.910
Endgame also echoes that series when ...shortly
after getting the scepter ...Captain America

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:05.849
2019 is forced to fight his double from 2012,
just as the real Steve Rogers defeated his

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:07.940
Hydra counterpart in Secret Empire.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.420
There are a lot of sad moments in Avengers:
Endgame's resolution, but one of the funniest

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.850
involves Thor and the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:18.690
Boarding the Benatar with a heavy sack, Thor
treats the space-faring crew as old friends.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:22.509
He slaps Star-Lord on the back and says, "Asgardians
of the Galaxy!

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:23.669
Back together again!"

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.370
Thor's reference to "Asgardians of the Galaxy"
is more than silly wordplay.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:32.210
In 2018, Marvel premiered the comic Asgardians
of the Galaxy, featuring Asgardian characters

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.400
on a space quest.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.970
The most recognizable characters to MCU fans
would be Skurge the Executioner, played by

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:42.650
Karl Urban in Thor: Ragnarok, and a younger
version of Loki, who begins the series hidden

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:43.790
in the Destroyer's armor.

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:48.700
There's also Valkyrie, Throg the Frog of Thunder,
and the Thor-like Earth hero Thunderstrike.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:52.780
Considering that Asgardians of the Galaxy
premiered in the second half of 2018, Thor's

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:57.390
line actually brings up a chicken-and-egg
question of exactly who is referencing whom?

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:01.690
Depending on exactly when the scene was filmed,
is Thor referencing the comic in that scene,

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:06.290
or was the Asgardians of the Galaxy comic
inspired by that line from the script?

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:10.330
Shortly after we see Cap leading his support
group, he visits Black Widow at the Avengers

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:11.330
compound.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.240
He talks with his old friend about moving
on from the purge, telling her:

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.400
"I keep telling everybody they should move
on.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.340
Some do… but not us."

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:25.850
Cap's talking about accepting the losses he
and Nat have suffered rather than trying to

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.630
find a way to reverse them, but there's a
bittersweet foreshadowing to the line as the

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:30.630
movie continues.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.500
By the time the credits roll, Captain America
and Black Widow are two characters who have

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:35.640
definitely moved on.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.850
Black Widow sacrifices herself to get the
Soul Stone from Vormir, while Steve Rogers

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:43.680
uses his quest to return the Infinity Stones
to recapture the life he lost when he plunged

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.620
into the ice in Captain America: The First
Avenger.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:50.950
If you weren’t already crying, that reveal
most likely triggered a few happy tears.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:54.160
"Taste it, taste the happy Michael.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.160
Tastes kinda like sad."

